1 of periods <italic>p<sub>x</sub></italic> = 2 <italic>&#x003bc;</italic>m and <italic>p<sub>y</sub></italic> = 3 <italic>&#x003bc;</italic>mThe grating transmission function is assumed to be a Ronchi grating. The Talbotdistance is <italic>Z<sub>T</sub></italic> = 232cm. (a) The distribution of the grating transmission function. (b) The distribution of the wave intensity at the Talbot distance. The spatial unit is <italic>&#x003bc;</italic>m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2008-827152.002"/></fig><fig id="fig3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Wave intensity distributions after a phase object. (a) Right behind the grating G1; (b) At the first Talbot distance; (c)  At the first Talbot distance by thefirst-order approximation. The spatial unit is <italic>&#x003bc;</italic>m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2008-827152.003"/></fig><fig id="fig4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Distributions of phase gradients.The phase object is specified in (<xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="eq24">24</xref>) with <italic>S<sub>x</sub></italic> = 5 and <italic>S<sub>y</sub></italic> = 5. (a) The true <italic>x</italic>-gradient; (c)  The measured <italic>x</italic>-gradient by phase stepping measurement. (b) and (d) are for the <italic>y</italic>-gradient. The spatial unit is <italic>&#x003bc;</italic>m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2008-827152.004"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>